1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,120
The Sun is getting brighter.

2
00:00:02,460 --> 00:00:04,960
Why does it brighten while it runs down?

3
00:00:05,460 --> 00:00:07,560
You are looking at the light on your face,

4
00:00:07,560 --> 00:00:10,660
and a prominence is already
lifting off the limb.

5
00:00:11,120 --> 00:00:12,880
The danger is not that ribbon.

6
00:00:13,100 --> 00:00:16,380
The danger is a slow climb
that is already true.

7
00:00:16,560 --> 00:00:18,120
It is not the spots.

8
00:00:18,200 --> 00:00:20,900
It is not the warming of a human century.

9
00:00:21,500 --> 00:00:25,180
In this film I will show
you why the light rises,

10
00:00:25,180 --> 00:00:30,500
what the sky was like under a fainter Sun,
and what happens as it keeps climbing.

11
00:00:30,820 --> 00:00:34,160
The motion is real, and
it is the wrong clock.

12
00:00:34,740 --> 00:00:38,500
A prominence is plasma held
in the Sun's magnetic field.

13
00:00:38,560 --> 00:00:41,740
It can hang, fall back, or break away.

14
00:00:41,900 --> 00:00:43,300
This one was over in a day.

15
00:00:43,880 --> 00:00:45,840
Your eyes are built for that.

16
00:00:45,960 --> 00:00:48,760
They are not built for
the change in the title.

17
00:00:49,280 --> 00:00:51,340
So is the Sun getting brighter?

18
00:00:51,660 --> 00:00:52,660
Yes.

19
00:00:52,660 --> 00:00:55,420
Not by a ribbon you could
watch from the garden.

20
00:00:55,420 --> 00:00:58,820
By a slow rise in the
light of the whole star.

21
00:00:59,120 --> 00:01:03,360
About one percent brighter in every
hundred and ten million years,

22
00:01:03,360 --> 00:01:05,280
as it uses its hydrogen.

23
00:01:06,340 --> 00:01:09,900
Schröder and Connon Smith
published that pace in 2008,

24
00:01:09,900 --> 00:01:12,760
in a model of the Sun's long life.

25
00:01:13,480 --> 00:01:15,880
What if the spots were the whole story?

26
00:01:16,040 --> 00:01:17,040
They are not.

27
00:01:17,040 --> 00:01:20,220
Across a cycle of about eleven years,

28
00:01:20,220 --> 00:01:24,280
the total light wobbles by
about a tenth of one percent.

29
00:01:24,720 --> 00:01:26,920
Spots darken a patch.

30
00:01:27,340 --> 00:01:30,940
Brighter faculae make up
the rest, and a little more.

31
00:01:31,100 --> 00:01:34,920
The wobble rises, falls, and comes back.

32
00:01:34,920 --> 00:01:36,500
It is not a climb.

33
00:01:37,200 --> 00:01:40,060
The warming measured on
Earth is a different ledger,

34
00:01:40,060 --> 00:01:42,180
written in the air over decades.

35
00:01:42,180 --> 00:01:45,560
This film does not say the
Sun has brightened enough,

36
00:01:45,560 --> 00:01:48,800
in your lifetime, to be that warming.

37
00:01:49,480 --> 00:01:55,020
Across a human life the one-percent climb
is far smaller than the sunspot wobble.

38
00:01:55,060 --> 00:01:58,200
You will not see it, and
you will not feel it.

39
00:01:58,680 --> 00:02:00,580
Now the strange question.

40
00:02:00,740 --> 00:02:04,520
Why would a star brighten
while its fuel is running down?

41
00:02:04,920 --> 00:02:06,620
The fuel is hydrogen.

42
00:02:06,760 --> 00:02:08,540
The ash is helium.

43
00:02:08,580 --> 00:02:13,580
The change sits in the core, where
the disc you see cannot show it.

44
00:02:13,840 --> 00:02:18,140
Deep inside, the Sun is
fusing hydrogen into helium.

45
00:02:18,560 --> 00:02:23,480
The net of the proton-proton chain
is simple enough to say aloud.

46
00:02:23,900 --> 00:02:28,180
Four hydrogen nuclei
become one helium nucleus.

47
00:02:28,400 --> 00:02:32,700
A little mass becomes energy,
and that energy is the light.

48
00:02:33,180 --> 00:02:37,300
The radiation that leaves the surface
takes eight minutes to reach you.

49
00:02:37,300 --> 00:02:40,660
The energy was made long before that.

50
00:02:41,080 --> 00:02:42,520
Here is the turn.

51
00:02:42,960 --> 00:02:45,800
Helium is not a second sort of hydrogen.

52
00:02:46,220 --> 00:02:50,980
Each helium nucleus locks up
more mass in fewer particles.

53
00:02:51,320 --> 00:02:53,560
As the core fills with ash,

54
00:02:53,560 --> 00:02:57,500
fewer particles remain to hold
the star up against gravity.

55
00:02:57,880 --> 00:03:01,320
Pressure would fall unless
something else rose.

56
00:03:01,540 --> 00:03:03,920
So the core contracts a little.

57
00:03:04,100 --> 00:03:05,460
It heats.

58
00:03:05,460 --> 00:03:08,320
Hotter hydrogen fuses faster.

59
00:03:08,540 --> 00:03:13,140
Faster fusion releases more
energy, and the star brightens.

60
00:03:13,500 --> 00:03:16,700
The Sun brightens because
it is running down.

61
00:03:16,920 --> 00:03:21,580
The waste in the middle leaves the
fire no choice but to burn harder.

62
00:03:21,940 --> 00:03:23,900
That is the first payoff.

63
00:03:24,180 --> 00:03:27,120
The tank is not dimming as it empties.

64
00:03:27,120 --> 00:03:31,760
About one percent more light in
every hundred and ten million years,

65
00:03:31,760 --> 00:03:34,760
for as long as this
remains the star you know.

66
00:03:34,860 --> 00:03:37,760
The spectrum drifts only a little hotter.

67
00:03:38,140 --> 00:03:42,640
What changes is the amount of
light, not a new colour on a walk.

68
00:03:43,300 --> 00:03:46,400
What would you see across
a hundred million years?

69
00:03:46,820 --> 00:03:51,780
Almost nothing at first, then a
Sun about one percent more severe.

70
00:03:52,120 --> 00:03:53,500
No mystery in it.

71
00:03:53,500 --> 00:03:56,600
Just mass, gravity and ash.

72
00:03:56,900 --> 00:03:59,240
We make one of these every week.

73
00:03:59,620 --> 00:04:02,380
Subscribing is how the next one finds you.

74
00:04:02,380 --> 00:04:07,900
But the Sun over your street is not
the Sun that a young Earth knew.

75
00:04:08,440 --> 00:04:09,880
Wind the clock back.

76
00:04:10,300 --> 00:04:13,820
When Earth was young, this
same star was fainter.

77
00:04:14,440 --> 00:04:19,200
Solar models put the early Sun near
seventy percent of today's light,

78
00:04:19,320 --> 00:04:23,400
roughly thirty percent dimmer
than the disc in your sky.

79
00:04:24,020 --> 00:04:26,560
The one-percent rule is today's pace.

80
00:04:26,720 --> 00:04:31,340
It is not a flat rate you can run backwards
across the whole life of the star.

81
00:04:31,500 --> 00:04:34,200
The young Sun was the dimmer one.

82
00:04:34,480 --> 00:04:36,480
Then the paradox arrives.

83
00:04:36,840 --> 00:04:40,860
A star that faint should have
left the early Earth frozen.

84
00:04:41,240 --> 00:04:43,120
The rocks say otherwise.

85
00:04:43,380 --> 00:04:45,040
There was liquid water.

86
00:04:45,120 --> 00:04:46,520
There was an ocean.

87
00:04:46,860 --> 00:04:50,240
How does a dimmer Sun
sit over a wet world?

88
00:04:50,820 --> 00:04:53,100
The usual repair is the air.

89
00:04:53,260 --> 00:04:57,620
More greenhouse gas, carbon
dioxide and early methane,

90
00:04:57,620 --> 00:05:01,220
held heat the weaker
sunlight did not supply.

91
00:05:01,520 --> 00:05:06,860
The faint young Sun is a paradox with
an answer, not a hole in the model.

92
00:05:07,300 --> 00:05:08,920
The sunlight was less.

93
00:05:08,920 --> 00:05:10,860
The atmosphere did more.

94
00:05:11,300 --> 00:05:15,420
How warm that early air was
is still partly unknown.

95
00:05:15,840 --> 00:05:18,140
What would you have seen from that shore?

96
00:05:18,420 --> 00:05:23,020
A Sun that gave less heat, a sky
that may not have been this blue,

97
00:05:23,020 --> 00:05:25,860
and a day that was not yet yours.

98
00:05:26,320 --> 00:05:29,920
The light was enough for water
once the air had taken its share.

99
00:05:29,920 --> 00:05:33,140
It was not the daylight
you are standing in.

100
00:05:34,000 --> 00:05:39,700
Until the two Suns sit side by side,
the daylight you trust feels finished.

101
00:05:39,960 --> 00:05:41,880
It is the middle of a climb.

102
00:05:42,580 --> 00:05:44,880
Now run today's pace forward.

103
00:05:44,880 --> 00:05:47,600
One percent in a hundred
and ten million years.

104
00:05:47,700 --> 00:05:52,340
Ten percent takes about ten of those
steps, a bit over a billion years.

105
00:05:52,980 --> 00:05:55,280
Schröder and Connon Smith
follow the rise and place

106
00:05:55,280 --> 00:05:58,580
a harder limit for Earth near
a billion years from now,

107
00:05:58,580 --> 00:06:01,540
when the Sun is on the order
of ten percent brighter.

108
00:06:02,080 --> 00:06:04,360
More light warms the surface.

109
00:06:04,520 --> 00:06:06,680
Warmer air holds more water.

110
00:06:06,740 --> 00:06:09,580
Water vapour is itself a greenhouse gas.

111
00:06:09,600 --> 00:06:12,960
Why the oceans would feel it
is that water in the air.

112
00:06:13,140 --> 00:06:16,480
Past the limit, they can
begin to lose water to space.

113
00:06:16,720 --> 00:06:19,880
The name for that threshold
is a moist greenhouse.

114
00:06:20,280 --> 00:06:22,320
It is not next century.

115
00:06:22,360 --> 00:06:25,280
It is the far side of the same climb.

116
00:06:25,640 --> 00:06:28,140
Could a shoreline survive it?

117
00:06:28,280 --> 00:06:30,420
Not one that still needs an ocean.

118
00:06:30,720 --> 00:06:34,300
But life under this star
has a very long while yet.

119
00:06:35,100 --> 00:06:37,460
The number matters
because of its direction.

120
00:06:37,620 --> 00:06:40,860
The Sun does not sit still
and then fail all at once.

121
00:06:40,860 --> 00:06:43,380
It leans brighter the whole way.

122
00:06:43,500 --> 00:06:46,780
The disc swells only slowly
through this stretch.

123
00:06:46,820 --> 00:06:48,220
A little larger.

124
00:06:48,680 --> 00:06:50,880
The thing that changes
the shore is the light.

125
00:06:50,880 --> 00:06:56,240
The great red swelling comes later,
and it belongs to another film.

126
00:06:56,380 --> 00:06:58,720
This one stops at the brightening.

127
00:06:58,820 --> 00:07:01,300
The blue can last a very long time.

128
00:07:01,340 --> 00:07:06,360
It cannot last for ever under
a star that only turns one way.

129
00:07:07,580 --> 00:07:11,840
However bright that future disc
is, you are not standing under it.

130
00:07:11,840 --> 00:07:14,300
You are standing in the
middle of the climb.

131
00:07:14,700 --> 00:07:16,540
The fainter Sun is behind you.

132
00:07:16,540 --> 00:07:21,300
The ten-percent Sun is further off
than any civilisation you can picture.

133
00:07:22,080 --> 00:07:26,660
Overhead is the star that learned to be
this bright, and it is still learning.

134
00:07:26,800 --> 00:07:30,300
About one percent every
hundred and ten million years.

135
00:07:30,440 --> 00:07:33,580
A tenth of one percent up
and down with the spots.

136
00:07:33,780 --> 00:07:36,420
A prominence when the cameras want motion.

137
00:07:37,040 --> 00:07:38,040
Three clocks.

138
00:07:38,040 --> 00:07:40,580
Only one of them answers the title.

139
00:07:41,300 --> 00:07:43,020
The Sun is getting brighter because the

140
00:07:43,020 --> 00:07:45,900
helium in its core leaves
the fire no choice.

141
00:07:46,220 --> 00:07:48,400
Earth has lived the dimmer half.

142
00:07:48,500 --> 00:07:52,380
The oceans are still here because
the climb has not gone too far.

143
00:07:52,500 --> 00:07:58,080
That is timing, and it is also just the
physics, running in ordinary daylight.

144
00:07:58,700 --> 00:08:02,380
What if the real question was
never whether the Sun is changing,

145
00:08:02,400 --> 00:08:06,840
but whether you arrived while the light
is still gentle enough for a blue sky?

146
00:08:07,340 --> 00:08:09,940
The bigger question sits in that blue.

147
00:08:10,100 --> 00:08:14,980
How long can it last, under a star
that brightens while it burns?

148
00:08:16,140 --> 00:08:20,060
Next door, there is a planet that
may already have taken that path.

149
00:08:20,240 --> 00:08:22,640
Next week: what happened to Venus.

150
00:08:22,640 --> 00:08:24,640
